          "question": "Burning when I pass urine, plus a temperature and back pain since yesterday. How urgent is this, and what does guidance describe for treatment?",
          "answer": "You need to seek medical attention promptly. Your symptoms—burning when passing urine, a temperature, and back pain—could be signs of a urinary tract infection (UTI) that may require immediate care, especially if you have a significant fever or flank pain.\n\nFor treatment, uncomplicated UTIs typically involve short-course antibiotic therapy, but this should align with local resistance patterns, and adjustments may be necessary for complicated cases. The presence of fever and severe back pain indicates a more serious infection, warranting urgent evaluation. While waiting for treatment, ensure you stay hydrated and manage your fever with supportive care.\n\nThere are some different opinions on how quickly antibiotics should be started for mild cystitis; some guidelines suggest a wait-and-see approach while others may recommend immediate antibiotic treatment, particularly for those at risk of complications. It is important to discuss your complete symptom profile with a healthcare provider to determine the best course of action.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
